 @steel5897  I mean Intel ARC has amazing ray tracing. So good in fact the performance hit isn't as massive as you'd nearly expect. I'd argue Intel's ray tracing could surpass Nvidia's in a few generations.

But yes, CUDA is a huge monopoly, and I think that if anyone could beat Nvidia it's Intel specifically because Intel holds huge market share in CPUs already, so ARC is something they can deploy with all of their OEMs very easily. Xe computing is going to be something Intel will heavily invest in and you bet that Intel won't stop at anything.

DLSS and RT are going to be answered. I think AMD's FSR 3.0 will easily match DLSS 3.0 and I think that their RDNA3 RT will be on par with RTX 3000 series at worst. We'll have to wait for reviews.

 @steel5897  Screens aren't getting "more and more" resolution. 4k is going to be the highest 99% of people go, especially gamers. 8k is a fluke and has been proven to be that, the only way it is going to be of any relevancy is on displays larger than 100 inches. Below that you can't tell a difference yet get a giant performance hit.

So no, AMD isn't close to catching up, they have already caught up. FSR is plenty good enough of a competitor to DLSS, not that you need either of those technologies on such insane GPUs, they already run 4k good enough, and at 1440p there's no question. RT is garbage, once you can run it with no performance loss, and is actually in more than like 50 games, cool lets have it. Until then, I'm saving my fps instead of gaining some barely noticeable lighting or reflection improvements. 

As for raw performance, I'm not sure if you're aware but AMD bones Nvidia at every price point with the 6000 series already, let alone the 7000 series. With the 6950xt slapping the 3090ti in quite a lot of games, for a lot less money

